From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:28:49 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Windows: honour FEX_HOSTFEATURES' disableavx on the iOS host

The iOS branch of FetchHostFeatures sets SupportsAVX for the ARM64EC host by
hand (fex-port 0064) and honours only the disable switches of the features it
sets itself (fex 0005, 0023), so there was no way to run an x86-64 title
without AVX: every one is told AVX, AVX2, FMA, F16C, BMI1/2 and XSAVE.

The ARM64EC contexts do not carry the ymm high halves: Wine's conversions drop
V[16..31] and never set CONTEXT_XSTATE, so an exception, APC or
Get/SetThreadContext context has no AVX state. A handler cannot read or set
the high halves, its own AVX writes (a vzeroupper, a memcpy built with AVX)
reach the code it resumes, and a fault continued in the middle of a block
resumes with the high half FEX last stored to its thread state, not the one
the block held in a register (the per-instruction flush covers only the GPR
and XMM static registers). Desktop Wine keeps all of it
(tools/compat/probes/ymmctx.c, every check ok). A title whose start is
exception-heavy, as World of Warcraft's is, can be run without AVX instead.

disableavx now clears SupportsAVX on this branch, after the hand-set value:
the guest sees what a CPU without AVX reports (no AVX, AVX2, FMA, F16C,
BMI1/2 or XSAVE in CPUID, XCR0 without AVX, IsProcessorFeaturePresent from
the same CPUID), and a VEX opcode decodes as an invalid instruction, as on
such a CPU. The disk cache keys its entries on the host features, so code
cached with AVX is not reused without it. The startup line already prints
AVX=. Nothing changes without the switch.

Linear: PLA-516
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-11-01-x64-ymm-contexts.md
Offered-upstream: no; the hand-built feature set exists only on the iOS port
---
 Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp b/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
index fd1b3a5..bc6a760 100644
--- a/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
+++ b/Source/Windows/Common/CPUFeatures.cpp
@@ -139,6 +139,17 @@ FEXCore::HostFeatures CPUFeatures::FetchHostFeatures(bool IsWine, FEXCore::HostF
       HostFeatures.SupportsSHA = false;
       HostFeatures.SupportsPMULL_128Bit = false;
     }
+    /* Playport: and AVX's, per launch, for a title whose AVX code must not meet
+     * the ARM64EC contexts: they carry the ymm high halves in no exception,
+     * APC or Get/SetThreadContext context (no CONTEXT_XSTATE), so a handler
+     * cannot read or set them and its own AVX writes reach the code it resumes,
+     * and a fault continued in the middle of a block can resume with a stale
+     * high half (tools/compat/probes/ymmctx.c). The guest then sees what a CPU
+     * without AVX reports: no AVX, AVX2, FMA, F16C, BMI1/2 or XSAVE in CPUID, no
+     * AVX in XCR0, and a VEX opcode is an invalid instruction. */
+    if (Control & FEXCore::Config::HostFeatures::DISABLEAVX) {
+      HostFeatures.SupportsAVX = false;
+    }
     LogMan::Msg::IFmt("FEX: iOS host AVX={} AFP={} LRCPC2={} RCPC={} atomics={} FlagM={} FlagM2={} FCMA={} crypto={} (FEX_HOSTFEATURES={})",
                       HostFeatures.SupportsAVX, HostFeatures.SupportsAFP, HostFeatures.SupportsTSOImm9, HostFeatures.SupportsRCPC,
                       HostFeatures.SupportsAtomics, HostFeatures.SupportsFlagM, HostFeatures.SupportsFlagM2,
